# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[rear]] part of the [[foot]], where it [[join]]s the [[leg]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1709|author={{w|John Denham (poet)|John Denham}}|title=s:Coopers-Hill
|passage=He [the stag] calls to mind his strength and then his speed, / His winged '''heels''' and then his armed head.}}
# The part of a [[shoe]]'s [[sole]] which [[support]]s the [[foot]]'s heel.
# The [[rear]] part of a [[sock]] or similar [[covering]] for the [[foot]].
# The part of the [[palm]] of a [[hand]] [[closest]] to the [[wrist]].
#: {{ux|en|He drove the '''heel''' of his hand into the man's nose.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} A [[high-heeled]] [[shoe]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Kwame Shauku|title=Wonderful Williams and the Magnificent Seven|page=257|passage=She'd been wearing '''heels''', and fell backward off her right '''heel''' and twisted or broke her ankle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Candace Irvine|title=A Dangerous Engagement| passage=Opting to improve her odds of making it up the stairs and into the privacy of her room, she kicked off her left '''heel''', and then her right before leaning down to scoop them up.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Alex Blackmore| title=Killing Eva| passage=Flat shoes. As she pushed off her left '''heel''' and pressed the sole of her foot to the cold floor she looked forward to them.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Chappell Roan;w:Dan Nigro|authorlabel=no|title=w:Pink Pony Club|date=3 April 2020|artist=Chappell Roan|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GR3Liudev18|text=Oh mama, I'm just having fun<br>On the stage in my '''heels'''<br>It's where I belong down at the...}}
# {{lb|en|firearms}} The [[back]], [[upper]] part of the [[stock]].
# {{lb|en|music}} The [[thickening]] of the [[neck]] of a [[stringed instrument]] where it [[attach]]es to the [[body]].
# The [[last]] or [[lowest]] part of anything.
#: {{ux|en|the '''heel''' of a mast}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''heel''' of a vessel}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author=w:Anthony Trollope|title=Framley Parsonage
|passage=And then again the sportsmen would move at an undertaker's pace, when the fox had traversed and the hounds would be at a loss to know which was the hunt and which was the '''heel'''}}
# {{lb|en|US|Ireland|Scotland|Australia}} A [[crust]] [[end]]-[[piece]] of a [[loaf]] of [[bread]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|passage=the '''heel''' of the white loaf}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1902|year_published=1980|author=w:Barbara Baynton|title=Bush Studies|series=Portable Australian Authors: Barbara Baynton|editors=Sally Krimmer; Alan Lawson|publisher=University of Queensland Press|location=St Lucia|page=32|passage=Boiled mutton was in one, and the '''heel''' of a damper in another.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=42|passage=`Have ye seen,' said Uncle Jobson with basilisk severity, `what's become of the '''heel''' of to-day's loaf?'}}
# {{lb|en|US}} The [[base]] of a [[bun]] [[slice]]d in [[half]] [[lengthwise]].
#: {{syn|en|boot|butt}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Ester Reiter|title=Making Fast Food: From the Frying Pan Into the Fryer|page=100|passage=The bottom half, or the bun '''heel''' is placed in the carton, and the pickle slices spread evenly over the meat or cheese.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|synecdochically}} A [[contemptible]], [[unscrupulous]], [[inconsiderate]]{{,}} or [[thoughtless]] person.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1953|author=w:Raymond Chandler|title=The Long Goodbye|chapter=29
|passage=I grinned at him sneeringly. I was the '''heel''' to end all '''heels'''. Wait until the man is down, then kick him and kick him again. He's weak. He can't resist or kick back.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=w:Philip Reeve|title=w:Scrivener's Moon|publisher={{w|Scholastic Corporation|Scholastic}}|isbn=978-1-407189-29-1|pages=361–362|text=She should be glad to have him on her side. So why did Godshawk's memories, grumbling again in that substrate of her brain, keep reminding her of '''heels''' he'd known, and tricksters?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jason Bailey|title=‘Basic Instinct’ at 30: A Time Capsule That Can Still Offend|work=The New York Times|date=2022-03-20|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/20/movies/basic-instinct-sharon-stone.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Douglas steams and stammers, a typical film noir '''heel''', while Stone delivers her dialogue with the devilish gleam of a sly actor having a great time.}}
# {{lb|en|by|extension|slang|professional wrestling}} A [[headline|headlining]] [[wrestler]] [[regard]]ed as a "[[bad guy]]," whose [[ring]] [[persona]] [[embody|embodies]] [[villainous]] or [[reprehensible]] [[trait]]s and [[demonstrate]]s [[characteristic]]s of a [[braggart]] and a [[bully]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Bruce Lincoln|title=Discourse and the Construction of Society|page=158|passage=Freedman began his analysis by noting two important facts about professional wrestling: First, that '''heels''' triumph considerably more often than do babyfaces{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|card games}} The [[card]]s [[set aside]] for [[later]] use in a [[patience]] or [[solitaire]] [[game]].
# Anything [[resemble|resembling]] a [[human]] heel in [[shape]]; a [[protuberance]]; a [[knob]].
# {{lb|en|architecture}} The [[lower]] [[end]] of a [[timber]] in a [[frame]], as a [[post]] or [[rafter]].
# {{lb|en|specifically|US}} The [[obtuse]] [[angle]] of the [[lower]] [[end]] of a [[rafter]] set [[sloping]].
# {{lb|en|architecture|obsolete}} A [[cyma reversa]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1722| title=A Treatise of the Five Orders in Architecture|author=Claude Perrault | page=vii| passage=Of these there are two Kinds; in the one, that Part which has the greatest ''Projecture'' is Concave, and is term'd ''Doucine'', or an Upright ''Ogee''; in the other, the Convex Part has the greatest ''Projecture''; and this is call'd the '''''Heel''''', or Inverted ''Ogee''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846| title=The Dictionary of the Arts, Sciences, and Manufactures|author=George William Francis | page=| passage=There are two kinds—the ''upright'' ogee, in which the concave part projects most, and the '''''heel''''' or ''inverted'' ogee, which has the convexity most prominent. This last, with its fillet above, is always the upper moulding of a classical cornice.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891| title=Practical Elementary Treatise on Architecture|author=Vignola | page=ii| passage=Talon: '''Heel''' moulding or ogee}}
# {{lb|en|carpentry}} The [[short]] [[side]] of an [[angle]]d [[cut]].
# {{lb|en|golf}} The part of a [[club]] [[head]]'s [[face]] [[near]]est the [[shaft]].
# The [[lower]] [[end]] of the [[bit]] ([[cut]]ting [[edge]]) of an [[axehead]], as opposed to the [[toe]] (upper end).
# The part of a [[carding machine]]'s [[flat]] [[near]]est the [[cylinder]].
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[junction]] between the [[keel]] and the [[stempost]] of a [[vessel]]; an [[angular]] [[wooden]] [[join]] [[connect]]ing the two.

